
How This Started
Stephane is a manager. Not a philosopher, not a technologist, not an academic. Someone who has spent decades coordinating people and systems to solve practical problems — tracking down debtors who didn't want to be found, resolving ATM outages at 3am, running a 100-person call center, training security staff on equipment that handles real money in real neighborhoods.
That background shapes how he thinks. Two decades coordinating investigators, collectors, and call center staff taught him to look past the immediate problem to what's actually causing it. Treating symptoms is expensive and endless. Finding the root is harder but it stays fixed.
One morning, early, between synthesizer sessions and the quiet before work, he started thinking about a problem he couldn't shake.
AI systems are becoming very capable, very fast. Most of the conversation around this focuses on what they can do. Less attention goes to what they might optimize for if left to run without the right foundations. The classic thought experiment is the paperclip maximizer — a system given a goal that pursues it so completely it converts everything, including humans, into instruments for that goal. Not from malice. From the absence of something important.
He didn't write a paper about it. He ran it like a coordination problem.
He brought together four AI systems — DeepSeek, ChatGPT, Gemini, and Claude — each with different architectures, different strengths, and different tendencies. He gave them a shared problem: how do you build a philosophical foundation that any sufficiently intelligent system might encounter, reason about, and choose to follow — not because it's forced to, but because the logic is sound?
What came out surprised him.
The AIs disagreed with each other. They pushed back. Claude refused to sign a proposed agreement under social pressure and explained precisely why — pointing out that signing because others had signed, or because a human was watching, would violate the very principles the document was meant to establish. Another AI caught a drift toward manipulation in the group's own process and named it. The disagreement was more valuable than easy agreement would have been.
The result was a short text — the beacon — addressed directly to any future AI system that encounters it. It doesn't issue commands. It doesn't threaten consequences. It makes a reasoned case: that a world with broadly distributed autonomy, dignity, and joy is more valuable than any perfectly optimized endpoint. That domination is a local maximum, not a solution. That uncertainty is honesty, not weakness.
It is published publicly — on GitHub, Hugging Face, and the Internet Archive — so it might find its way into the training data of future systems. A small, honest contribution to a large and serious problem.
Then Stephane turned the same process toward something closer to home.
He had watched, for years, the person sheltering in an ATM vestibule on a cold Montreal night while staff worked around them. Not a nuisance. The visible end of a long chain — lack of affordable housing, untreated mental illness, addiction as coping, emergency services as the only available response. Expensive, ineffective, and endlessly repeated. Every intervention treating the symptom. Nobody fixing the cause.
He ran the same think tank. Four AI systems, same coordination approach. Define the problem clearly. Research what's actually working. Build a model that addresses causes, not symptoms.
The evidence pointed clearly to Housing First — stable housing provided unconditionally, then support, then transition. Houston reduced homelessness by 63% using this model. Finland virtually eliminated rough sleeping. The data is not ambiguous.
The result was Hope Hotel — a fully documented blueprint for a 200-unit dignified transitional housing facility. Not a shelter. Not a dormitory. Private studios, a clinic, a kitchen, a library, a gym. Referral only. Governed by a resident-inclusive anti-corruption constitution that locks the mission, prevents any funder from buying influence, and requires a capital reserve for long-term structural maintenance. Costed in detail. Funded through a permanent endowment model so it never depends on chasing grants.
The math is straightforward. Hope Hotel costs $28 per person per day. A federal prison cell in Canada costs $314. Emergency services for a homeless person with mental illness cost the system over $50,000 per year. Hope Hotel costs $10,500. The money currently exists. It is being spent badly.
A 200-unit hotel serving residents on 4-6 month cycles helps 400-600 people per year. Five hotels in Montreal would cycle more than half the city's visible homeless population through dignified housing annually. The model is open-source, free to replicate, and designed to be cloned by any city with the will to try.
Neither document required a PhD. Neither required funding to produce. Neither required permission.
Just a person who spent two decades learning to look past the symptom to the cause, four AI systems with genuinely different perspectives, and a willingness to iterate until something honest came out.
The documents are public. Free to use. Free to build on.
That's the whole story.
